      "label": "Separation r (Bohr radii, a₀ = 5.29×10⁻¹¹ m)",
      "range": [0.5, 3.0, 0.05],
      "default": 1.0,
      "teaches": "Force magnitude obeys the inverse square law — halving r quadruples the arrow length while its direction stays exactly radial."
    },
    {
      "id": "orbitView",
      "type": "orbit",
      "teaches": "The electron really is off the equatorial plane in depth; rotating shows the dashed guides are a 3D coordinate drop, not a flat triangle."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Ground state, as drawn",
      "narration": "The proton sits at the center of the sphere and the electron rides on the shell at the most probable distance, one Bohr radius away. The black segment r and the red arrow F lie along the same line, but F points inward.",
      "state": { "separation": 1.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the electron around the shell",
      "narration": "Drag the electron anywhere on the sphere. The meridian arc, the dashed drop lines, r and F all follow — F never stops pointing at the proton, because the Coulomb force is central.",
      "state": { "separation": 1.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pull the electron farther out",
      "narration": "At r = 2a₀ the direction of F is unchanged but the arrow shrinks to one quarter of its length: F = ke²/r².",
      "state": { "separation": 2.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Squeeze it closer",
      "narration": "At r = 0.5a₀ the arrow grows four times longer — the attraction climbs steeply as the electron approaches the nucleus.",
      "state": { "separation": 0.5 }
    }
  ],
  "geometry_notes": "World scale: outer sphere radius R = 2.0 units, centered at the origin; this R corresponds to one Bohr radius a₀ = 5.29×10⁻¹¹ m at the slider default of 1.0 (so slider value s places the electron at radius 2.0·s units, and the sphere itself should rescale with s so the electron always rides ON the drawn shell). Sphere drawn as a translucent/wireframe silhouette only: grey great-circle outline #B0B0B0, ~2 px. Equatorial ellipse lies in the y = 0 plane (the XZ plane), drawn pale grey #CFCFCF; from the standard axonometric pose it reads as a wide flat ellipse about 4.0 units wide and ~0.8 units tall on screen, i.e. viewed from roughly 12–15° above the plane — the camera is only slightly above the equator. Proton: dark navy disc #16466E with a thin lighter blue ring and a white '+' glyph, radius ~0.16 units, at the origin. Electron: pale steel-blue sphere #7FB3D5 with slightly darker outline, radius ~0.10 units, positioned upper-right at azimuth ≈ 35° toward the viewer's right-front and elevation ≈ 42° above the equatorial plane (screen position: right of the vertical dashed axis, roughly two-thirds of the way up the sphere). Meridian arc: medium blue #2E86AB, ~1.5 px, running along the sphere surface from the north pole (0, +R, 0) down the electron's own meridian to the electron — it curves down and to the right. Segment r: solid black ~1.5 px from proton to electron; label 'r' in italic serif placed just above-left of the segment's upper half. Force arrow F: thick dark red #A92E22 (~5 px shaft with a broad filled arrowhead) starting at the electron and ending short of the proton, pointing exactly antiparallel to r; label 'F' in italic serif just below-right of the arrow's midpoint. Vertical dashed line: black, dash ~0.12/0.10, along +Y and −Y through the center, extending ~0.6 units beyond the sphere top and reaching the bottom of the sphere. Horizontal dashed line: black, along the screen-horizontal diameter of the equatorial ellipse, extending slightly past the ellipse on both sides. Guide lines from the electron: light blue #9FC8E0 dashed — one vertical drop from the electron straight down (−Y) to its foot point on the equatorial plane, and one from that foot point back to the center along the plane. Coulomb value for correctness: F = k e²/r² with k = 8.99×10⁹ N·m²/C², e = 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ C; at r = a₀ = 5.29×10⁻¹¹ m this gives F ≈ 8.25×10⁻⁸ N. Draw the arrow length proportional to 1/s² with the default (s = 1) length ≈ 0.55·R, clamped to ≤ 0.9·R so it never overshoots the proton.",
  "notes": "Build the sphere silhouette with a THREE.LineLoop great circle that is re-oriented each frame to face the camera (or a MeshBasicMaterial sphere with very low opacity plus an EdgesGeometry-free outline ring) so it always reads as a clean circle during orbit. Equatorial ellipse = flat circle of radius R in the XZ plane drawn as a line loop. Dashed lines need LineDashedMaterial with computeLineDistances(). The red F arrow is best as a custom mesh (CylinderGeometry shaft + ConeGeometry head) grouped and oriented with quaternion setFromUnitVectors so it can rescale in length without distorting the head. Labels 'r' and 'F' as CSS2DRenderer/sprite labels anchored to the segment midpoint and offset a few pixels so they never overlap the arrow. Drag: raycast the pointer against an invisible sphere of radius R and snap the electron to the hit point; suppress OrbitControls while the pointer is down on the electron (set controls.enabled = false on pointerdown over the electron). Recompute the meridian arc as a QuadraticBezier/spherical interpolation between the north pole and the electron each drag frame. Slider should scale the whole shell, the electron radius position, the guide lines and the arrow together in one update function. Render order: dashed guides first, then sphere outline, then r segment, then arrow, then charges on top with depthTest disabled for the proton/electron discs so they stay visible through the shell."
}

