CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a schematic three-dimensional depiction of a hydrogen atom drawn as one large sphere. The sphere is rendered as a grey outline circle with a paler grey ellipse across its middle standing for an equatorial plane, which gives the drawing depth. At the center sits a small dark blue disc bearing a plus sign, representing the proton. A second, much smaller pale blue sphere representing the electron is placed on the upper right of the spherical surface, and a thin blue arc curves down to it from the top of the sphere. A straight black line labeled r joins the central charge to the electron, and a thick red arrow labeled F lies along that same line, pointing from the electron back toward the nucleus. A vertical dashed line through the center, a horizontal dashed line across the equatorial ellipse, and faint blue dashed guides dropping from the electron to that ellipse fix the electron's position in three dimensions. Drawing the force antiparallel to r conveys at a glance that the interaction is attractive and directed along the line joining the two particles. The sketch sets up a Coulomb's law calculation of the force on the ground-state electron at its most probable distance from the proton.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - large outline sphere (grey great-circle silhouette) representing the hydrogen atom / electron's spherical shell
  - pale grey equatorial ellipse across the middle of the sphere (equatorial plane seen edge-on-ish)
  - small dark-blue disc with a white '+' sign at the sphere's center — the proton (nucleus)
  - small pale-blue sphere on the upper-right of the spherical surface — the electron
  - thin blue meridian arc curving from the top (north pole) of the sphere down to the electron
  - straight black line segment from proton to electron, labeled 'r'
  - thick red arrow lying along that same line, tail at the electron, head pointing back toward the proton, labeled 'F'
  - vertical black dashed line through the sphere center (polar axis), extending above and below the sphere
  - horizontal black dashed line across the equatorial ellipse through the center
  - faint blue dashed vertical drop line from the electron down to the equatorial plane
  - faint blue dashed radial line in the equatorial plane from the center out to the foot of that drop line
  - italic text labels 'r' (above the segment) and 'F' (below/right of the red arrow)

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World scale: outer sphere radius R = 2.0 units, centered at the origin; this R corresponds to one Bohr radius a₀ = 5.29×10⁻¹¹ m at the slider default of 1.0 (so slider value s places the electron at radius 2.0·s units, and the sphere itself should rescale with s so the electron always rides ON the drawn shell). Sphere drawn as a translucent/wireframe silhouette only: grey great-circle outline #B0B0B0, ~2 px. Equatorial ellipse lies in the y = 0 plane (the XZ plane), drawn pale grey #CFCFCF; from the standard axonometric pose it reads as a wide flat ellipse about 4.0 units wide and ~0.8 units tall on screen, i.e. viewed from roughly 12–15° above the plane — the camera is only slightly above the equator. Proton: dark navy disc #16466E with a thin lighter blue ring and a white '+' glyph, radius ~0.16 units, at the origin. Electron: pale steel-blue sphere #7FB3D5 with slightly darker outline, radius ~0.10 units, positioned upper-right at azimuth ≈ 35° toward the viewer's right-front and elevation ≈ 42° above the equatorial plane (screen position: right of the vertical dashed axis, roughly two-thirds of the way up the sphere). Meridian arc: medium blue #2E86AB, ~1.5 px, running along the sphere surface from the north pole (0, +R, 0) down the electron's own meridian to the electron — it curves down and to the right. Segment r: solid black ~1.5 px from proton to electron; label 'r' in italic serif placed just above-left of the segment's upper half. Force arrow F: thick dark red #A92E22 (~5 px shaft with a broad filled arrowhead) starting at the electron and ending short of the proton, pointing exactly antiparallel to r; label 'F' in italic serif just below-right of the arrow's midpoint. Vertical dashed line: black, dash ~0.12/0.10, along +Y and −Y through the center, extending ~0.6 units beyond the sphere top and reaching the bottom of the sphere. Horizontal dashed line: black, along the screen-horizontal diameter of the equatorial ellipse, extending slightly past the ellipse on both sides. Guide lines from the electron: light blue #9FC8E0 dashed — one vertical drop from the electron straight down (−Y) to its foot point on the equatorial plane, and one from that foot point back to the center along the plane. Coulomb value for correctness: F = k e²/r² with k = 8.99×10⁹ N·m²/C², e = 1.602×10⁻¹⁹ C; at r = a₀ = 5.29×10⁻¹¹ m this gives F ≈ 8.25×10⁻⁸ N. Draw the arrow length proportional to 1/s² with the default (s = 1) length ≈ 0.55·R, clamped to ≤ 0.9·R so it never overshoots the proton.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "dragElectron",
    "type": "drag",
    "teaches": "The Coulomb force is a central force: wherever the electron sits on the shell, r and F stay along the same line and F always points back at the proton — the interaction is attractive and radial, independent of direction."
  },
  {
    "id": "separation",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Separation r (Bohr radii, a₀ = 5.29×10⁻¹¹ m)",
