      0.05
    ],
    "default": 1,
    "teaches": "Force magnitude obeys the inverse square law — halving r quadruples the arrow length while its direction stays exactly radial."
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitView",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The electron really is off the equatorial plane in depth; rotating shows the dashed guides are a 3D coordinate drop, not a flat triangle."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "Ground state, as drawn",
    "narration": "The proton sits at the center of the sphere and the electron rides on the shell at the most probable distance, one Bohr radius away. The black segment r and the red arrow F lie along the same line, but F points inward.",
    "state": {
      "separation": 1
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Move the electron around the shell",
    "narration": "Drag the electron anywhere on the sphere. The meridian arc, the dashed drop lines, r and F all follow — F never stops pointing at the proton, because the Coulomb force is central.",
    "state": {
      "separation": 1
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Pull the electron farther out",
    "narration": "At r = 2a₀ the direction of F is unchanged but the arrow shrinks to one quarter of its length: F = ke²/r².",
    "state": {
      "separation": 2
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Squeeze it closer",
    "narration": "At r = 0.5a₀ the arrow grows four times longer — the attraction climbs steeply as the electron approaches the nucleus.",
    "state": {
      "separation": 0.5
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "rollDeg": 0,
  "heightFraction": 0.7
}

NOTES: Build the sphere silhouette with a THREE.LineLoop great circle that is re-oriented each frame to face the camera (or a MeshBasicMaterial sphere with very low opacity plus an EdgesGeometry-free outline ring) so it always reads as a clean circle during orbit. Equatorial ellipse = flat circle of radius R in the XZ plane drawn as a line loop. Dashed lines need LineDashedMaterial with computeLineDistances(). The red F arrow is best as a custom mesh (CylinderGeometry shaft + ConeGeometry head) grouped and oriented with quaternion setFromUnitVectors so it can rescale in length without distorting the head. Labels 'r' and 'F' as CSS2DRenderer/sprite labels anchored to the segment midpoint and offset a few pixels so they never overlap the arrow. Drag: raycast the pointer against an invisible sphere of radius R and snap the electron to the hit point; suppress OrbitControls while the pointer is down on the electron (set controls.enabled = false on pointerdown over the electron). Recompute the meridian arc as a QuadraticBezier/spherical interpolation between the north pole and the electron each drag frame. Slider should scale the whole shell, the electron radius position, the guide lines and the arrow together in one update function. Render order: dashed guides first, then sphere outline, then r segment, then arrow, then charges on top with depthTest disabled for the proton/electron discs so they stay visible through the shell.

